 behaviour, so no test could catch it. They fall into these groups:\n- **Comparisons that make no difference:** changing `day > maxdays` to `>=` in `Duration` gives the same date either way.\n- **Branches that can never run:**\n  - In `parse_duration`, the alternative format only builds a timedelta when the year is 0, which a Python date can't have.\n  - `parse_tzinfo` can never raise its error, because its regex also matches an empty string.\n  - The fallback lines in the strftime replacement functions can't be reached, because the regex only matches codes they already handle.\n- **Values that are set and then overwritten:** the `Decimal(0)` starting values in `parse_time`, and the `expanded = True` in `parse_date`, which `build_date_regexps` forces again anyway.\n- **Changes with no visible effect:** `rstrip(\"XX0XX\")` strips the same characters as `rstrip(\"0\")`; `\"0n\"` vs `\"0N\"` only changes a letter that gets cut off; a sign multiplier that only applies to years Python can't represent; and defaults that are always overridden.\n\n**What the tests cover:**\n- Every date, time and timezone format the parsers accept, in basic and extended forms, with 4-digit and 6-digit (signed) years. This includes week dates in years where 1 January falls in week 1 and in years where it doesn't.\n- Parsing of fractional hours, minutes and seconds, including the round-down rule for microseconds.\n- Formatting of dates, times and durations with every format code.\n- The `Duration` class: maths, equality, hashing, `str`/`repr`, pickling, month-end and leap-year clamping, and `totimedelta`.\n- `tz_isoformat` edge cases, plus `FixedOffset`, `UTC`, and `LocalTimezone` (with a fake `time` module so the DST tests don't depend on the machine's timezone).\n- Exact error messages.\n\n**Regex caches:** the library caches its compiled regexes at module level. Without intervention, mutants in the regex-building code would never actually run during mutation testing, and about 90 of them survived for that reason. An autouse fixture in the test file clears both caches before each test.\n\nRunning the tests left an untracked `isodate/__pycache__/` folder, which you can delete.","ttft_ms":3066,"type":"result","duration_ms":494490,"uuid":"47b97b4f-ffc1-495b-8a04-103998828f33","ttft_stream_ms":1880,"time_to_request_ms":192,"first_content_frame_ms":1880,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

